// Which channel a native fallible holds, asked without unwrapping it.
// A native fallible `T ! E` is a compiler-owned form with no methods of its own, so the questions every caller asks of
// one live here as ordinary generic functions. Each takes the outcome by value, which is what lets a call's result be
// asked directly; a named outcome whose payloads cannot be copied is passed as `<-outcome`, and is then gone.
// Asking is not handling. A caller that needs the success or the failure matches `.Success(value)` and
// `.Failure(error)`, propagates with `?`, or recovers with `catch`; these two are for a branch or an assertion that
// needs only the answer.

/// Whether `outcome` holds a success.
/// `outcome` is consumed; its payload is destroyed after the question is answered. Runs in constant time and
/// allocates nothing.
/// @typeParam T the success type
/// @typeParam E the error type
/// @param outcome the fallible to ask
/// @returns whether it holds a success
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/core/outcomes#succeeded
pub func Succeeded<T, E>(outcome: T ! E) -> bool {
    return match outcome {
        .Success(_) => true,
        .Failure(_) => false
    };
}

/// Whether `outcome` holds a failure.
/// `outcome` is consumed; its payload is destroyed after the question is answered. Runs in constant time and
/// allocates nothing.
/// @typeParam T the success type
/// @typeParam E the error type
/// @param outcome the fallible to ask
/// @returns whether it holds a failure
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/core/outcomes#failed
pub func Failed<T, E>(outcome: T ! E) -> bool {
    return match outcome {
        .Success(_) => false,
        .Failure(_) => true
    };
}
